00:32:26.860 --> 00:32:28.580
like we're just gonna have
so much fun with that.

00:32:28.580 --> 00:32:32.290
But you also can't get
so engrossed in that.

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:34.160
It's very easy to have
New Year's resolutions,

00:32:34.160 --> 00:32:35.780
it's totally different thing to execute

00:32:35.780 --> 00:32:37.373
and to actually stick with something.

00:32:37.373 --> 00:32:39.490
A habit isn't a habit 'cause
you called it a habit,

00:32:39.490 --> 00:32:41.940
it's a habit because
it becomes a habitual.

00:32:41.940 --> 00:32:46.940
So I think that's a lot
of what's in front of me.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.270
I mean, like is helping people understand

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:50.840
that they actually have the time

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:52.160
to do the things that they want to do

00:32:52.160 --> 00:32:53.810
if they care about it that much

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:56.370
and, Gary, you've talked
about this ad nauseam,

00:32:56.370 --> 00:32:58.030
how late are you watching
Netflix at night,

00:32:58.030 --> 00:32:59.270
what could you be doing?

00:32:59.270 --> 00:33:02.610
By the way, it's very easy
to get seven hours of sleep

00:33:02.610 --> 00:33:04.290
if, you know, you wanna wake up early

00:33:04.290 --> 00:33:06.203
and do the thing that you want to do.

00:33:06.203 --> 00:33:09.250
And I have such a totally
different perspective,

00:33:09.250 --> 00:33:10.790
I think about where I was 10 years ago,

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:14.227
it's probably when I discovered
Gary, read Crush It in 2009,

00:33:14.227 --> 00:33:16.640
totally different place in my life.

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:19.250
I've had tragedy happen, I've
had beautiful things happen,

00:33:19.250 --> 00:33:21.660
I've had high of highs happen,
I just wrote my second book

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:22.940
which is really weird.

00:33:22.940 --> 00:33:26.676
I'm very excited to be able
to take what I've learned,

00:33:26.676 --> 00:33:29.070
especially in the last couple of years

00:33:29.070 --> 00:33:31.033
into the next stage in my life

00:33:31.033 --> 00:33:32.473
because I think it's the best place

00:33:32.473 --> 00:33:33.370
that my headspace could be right now.

00:33:33.370 --> 00:33:34.950
To be realistic about the fact

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:37.630
that we're so lucky to breathe every day.

00:33:37.630 --> 00:33:42.611
- Yeah, there is no
micro event, Resy selling

00:33:42.611 --> 00:33:45.060
or Vayner being positioned for a big year

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:48.350
or a sneaker deal, a car
deal, a wine company,

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:52.540
like there's no micro
event, even family stuff,

00:33:52.540 --> 00:33:55.390
kids getting older, you know, family time,

00:33:55.390 --> 00:33:57.300
even that I call a micro event.

00:33:57.300 --> 00:34:01.013
I don't celebrate because
I'm always celebrating.

00:34:01.013 --> 00:34:05.270
I don't celebrate micro,
I truly celebrate macro.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:07.200
I'm literally one of these people,

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:08.960
you know, it's funny, I'm not religious

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:11.430
but I'm definitely spiritual.

00:34:11.430 --> 00:34:13.550
Like I literally wake up and I'm like--

00:34:13.550 --> 00:34:15.470
- [Calvin] You have not talked
about this enough, Gary,

00:34:15.470 --> 00:34:16.410
I just have to say.

00:34:16.410 --> 00:34:18.700
- Yeah, I agree with you.

00:34:18.700 --> 00:34:21.010
- [Calvin] The spiritual
side and things like that.

00:34:21.010 --> 00:34:22.020
- Because you know what's funny,

00:34:22.020 --> 00:34:23.663
because you know what's funny?

00:34:23.663 --> 00:34:24.800
I'm trying to find different
angles to say the same thing

00:34:24.800 --> 00:34:26.570
'cause I want, I'm
hoping to use something,

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:27.980
even the way you're reacting, and Calvin,

00:34:27.980 --> 00:34:29.840
you're on your shit if you knew 202

00:34:29.840 --> 00:34:31.230
and you knew Amy asked me that question

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:33.840
so you're consuming and you
have context and you're right,

00:34:33.840 --> 00:34:35.760
I don't know if I've
ever even used spiritual,

00:34:35.760 --> 00:34:39.450
I think maybe one other time
if I'm like kind of feeling it.

00:34:39.450 --> 00:34:43.590
The reason I'm saying that is
I don't think people hear me.

00:34:43.590 --> 00:34:46.670
I really mean it when I say
that I woke up this morning

00:34:46.670 --> 00:34:48.540
and I was like you, phew,

00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:49.940
no phone call in the middle of the night

00:34:49.940 --> 00:34:51.700
that I have to run to the hospital.

00:34:51.700 --> 00:34:54.740
Phew, super pumped I'm
not in heaven right now

00:34:54.740 --> 00:34:56.597
'cause that, you know
that, brain hemorrhage--

00:34:56.597 --> 00:34:58.450
- Sounds like a great place, yeah.

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:02.701
- Like, you know, like I don't celebrate

00:35:02.701 --> 00:35:04.590
because I'm actually celebrating

00:35:04.590 --> 00:35:06.400
and it's kind of how I
think about meditating

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:08.830
and it's how I kind of
think about balance.

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:11.160
Like, I've got some gear inside of me

00:35:11.160 --> 00:35:13.620
that has a deep perspective

00:35:13.620 --> 00:35:16.240
that puts me in such a good place

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:20.100
that I'm celebrating everything
and nothing, you know,

00:35:20.100 --> 00:35:23.523
and that's that, like I don't
know, like everything's--

00:35:23.523 --> 00:35:24.927
- [Calvin] But the thing is with you

00:35:24.927 --> 00:35:25.760
that's so interesting is,

00:35:25.760 --> 00:35:27.881
what's so interesting about you though

00:35:27.881 --> 00:35:30.860
is a lot of people get that
from something, usually,

00:35:30.860 --> 00:35:34.147
they usually get that from their religion,

00:35:34.147 --> 00:35:38.246
maybe they found some other
guru or they read something,

00:35:38.246 --> 00:35:39.200
you haven't done any of that shit.

00:35:39.200 --> 00:35:41.164
- I found it within myself.

00:35:41.164 --> 00:35:42.350
You know what's funny?

00:35:42.350 --> 00:35:44.810
I've been thinking about
lately this thought.

00:35:44.810 --> 00:35:49.810
Was I so, I cried so
much from one to eight,

00:35:50.820 --> 00:35:55.820
from zero to eight Gary
Vaynerchuk cried every day.

00:35:55.840 --> 00:36:00.840
I was so emotional, my new
hypothesis is the following.

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:06.530
Huh, I am so empathetic,
and when I say crying,

00:36:06.530 --> 00:36:09.450
when I was sad, when my mom was sad,

00:36:09.450 --> 00:36:11.740
when I saw somebody else was sad,

00:36:11.740 --> 00:36:15.210
like watching a movie,
TV, I didn't like the way

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.450
that person got picked on
in first grade, I cried.

00:36:17.450 --> 00:36:19.110
Like literally this is
scene in my head right now,

00:36:19.110 --> 00:36:20.920
somebody get, I know what it is,

00:36:20.920 --> 00:36:24.690
a kid got picked on in class,
I see it, I just start crying.

00:36:24.690 --> 00:36:29.200
My belief is that I
might be so empathetic.

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:32.530
- [Amy] You like people a lot.

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:36.930
- That I think I started
building a reverse behavior

00:36:36.930 --> 00:36:41.930
of detachment to even get
through by being so empathetic

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:46.740
that I created some sort
of thing within myself

00:36:46.740 --> 00:36:51.180
that made me go the complete
other way to make life palpable

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:53.930
and I practiced it so hard

00:36:53.930 --> 00:36:57.400
and that I probably was
naturally born so empathetic

00:36:57.400 --> 00:37:00.710
and that my counter-reaction was to

00:37:00.710 --> 00:37:03.390
detach myself from those feelings

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:05.880
that I'm now playing at such a bridge of,

00:37:05.880 --> 00:37:08.483
I don't give a fuck, I
care about everybody.

00:37:09.550 --> 00:37:11.960
- This is what I've always
been confused about with you

00:37:11.960 --> 00:37:14.900
'cause I feel like, I operate
at very similar levels

00:37:14.900 --> 00:37:17.480
than you all the time but
I am 100% introverted.

00:37:17.480 --> 00:37:19.390
I know how to be on when I need to be on,

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:21.250
I love connecting with
people in a conversation,

00:37:21.250 --> 00:37:23.035
I don't need to look at my phone--

00:37:23.035 --> 00:37:24.600
- I would I would argue I have
introverted characteristics,

00:37:24.600 --> 00:37:26.813
I'm wildly good at being by myself.

00:37:28.670 --> 00:37:30.680
- [Amy] You're both,
you're 100% percent both.

00:37:30.680 --> 00:37:33.840
- And I'm real extreme on both too.

00:37:33.840 --> 00:37:36.993
Like I think I recognize
that in you too and like my,

00:37:38.023 --> 00:37:39.700
and it's not better or worse,

00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:41.910
I think you might be
here and I might be here

00:37:41.910 --> 00:37:43.240
and in that is even something

00:37:43.240 --> 00:37:44.290
'cause I definitely know what happens

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:45.890
in between this and this,

00:37:45.890 --> 00:37:48.190
that's my that's my theory right now.

00:37:48.190 --> 00:37:50.120
I think I got it from within myself

00:37:51.733 --> 00:37:54.110
out of a correction of maybe being

00:37:54.110 --> 00:37:56.730
too feelings oriented early on

00:37:56.730 --> 00:37:58.580
and then I think it became patterns

00:37:58.580 --> 00:38:00.190
and building and building and building.

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:04.340
I'm telling you, I couldn't
explain to everybody here

00:38:04.340 --> 00:38:07.620
enough of how much crying I did

00:38:07.620 --> 00:38:09.320
in the first five to
eight years of my life,

00:38:09.320 --> 00:38:10.780
I was constantly crying.

00:38:10.780 --> 00:38:15.500
I cried every time I lost in
anything until I was like 15.

00:38:15.500 --> 00:38:17.830
It's weird when you're
13 with your buddies,

00:38:17.830 --> 00:38:19.100
you lose in like, you know,

00:38:19.100 --> 00:38:23.470
like in like Nintendo football
and you're like crying

00:38:23.470 --> 00:38:28.470
and like punching walls,
I'm hyper on emotionally

00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:32.750
and I think to get through I created--

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:34.810
- Well, you learned that
it didn't do anything

00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:35.643
it sounds like.

00:38:35.643 --> 00:38:37.460
- [Gary] Yeah, or like it was exhausting.

00:38:37.460 --> 00:38:40.145
- Yeah, it's like okay,
so, we're still doing that

00:38:40.145 --> 00:38:40.978
and it's not doing anything.

00:38:40.978 --> 00:38:42.162
- It's funny that when you just said that

00:38:42.162 --> 00:38:43.010
like I went into a place of like, no,

00:38:43.010 --> 00:38:44.899
I think it was exhausting.

00:38:44.899 --> 00:38:46.963
And I think that's why I am I am.

00:38:48.006 --> 00:38:52.770
- [Calvin] So to tie that to
goal setting, you mentioned--

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:54.510
- I want to be the number
one person on Earth,

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:58.840
I don't give a fuck if
nothing happens, that's it.

00:38:58.840 --> 00:39:01.500
- [Calvin] So you're in that good balance.

00:39:01.500 --> 00:39:03.220
So when you have a new year,

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:04.790
so you have this big macro thing

00:39:04.790 --> 00:39:08.010
of some day buying the New
York Jets and there's that

00:39:08.010 --> 00:39:09.850
and then you're in the
trenches every day--

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:12.980
- But that's a front for me
just to keep playing forever.

00:39:12.980 --> 00:39:14.190
- It's so easy to latch on to.

00:39:14.190 --> 00:39:17.255
It's not the thing.
- It's like a least thing,

00:39:17.255 --> 00:39:18.520
the thing is the process
of trying and then--

00:39:18.520 --> 00:39:20.300
- It's like, here's why
I'm still going, guys,

00:39:20.300 --> 00:39:21.200
still don't own the Jets.

00:39:21.200 --> 00:39:24.940
- [Calvin] So here's my
question, my question is,

00:39:24.940 --> 00:39:26.300
with a new year coming up,

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:28.360
I know you usually take time
off at the end of the year,

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:33.360
are you actually planning
and making up goals for 2020

00:39:33.390 --> 00:39:35.680
or are you just like, what's
fuckin' comin' on Monday?

00:39:35.680 --> 00:39:36.760
- No, that's right.

00:39:36.760 --> 00:39:40.700
But I'm a plan the next
15 years on an insight

00:39:40.700 --> 00:39:44.880
that I pick up on March
16th walking down to my car

00:39:44.880 --> 00:39:47.130
at 6:03 p.m. during a rainstorm

00:39:47.130 --> 00:39:49.330
and might say, wait a minute.

00:39:49.330 --> 00:39:52.760
I'm in complete and utter
motion at all times.

00:39:52.760 --> 00:39:54.790
I'm in strategy all the time,

00:39:54.790 --> 00:39:58.180
I don't sit down and write
out it on paper and say,

00:39:58.180 --> 00:40:00.460
oh these last three years, and by the way,

00:40:00.460 --> 00:40:02.520
that's my strength and my weakness.

00:40:02.520 --> 00:40:06.840
I just still think I'm
early, I eat my own advice.

00:40:06.840 --> 00:40:10.770
I believe at 44 years old
that I'm not in panic mode

00:40:10.770 --> 00:40:12.213
on my great ambitions.

00:40:13.930 --> 00:40:16.010
- [Calvin] Love it, how about you, Amy?

00:40:16.010 --> 00:40:18.720
- I mean, like I look at
my goals every single day

00:40:18.720 --> 00:40:20.130
and they mattered they matter to me,

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:21.200
I'm rewriting them every day

00:40:21.200 --> 00:40:23.040
but at the same time I feel like

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:24.560
they're my version of the Jets.

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:28.070
Like I enjoy everything
I'm doing on a daily basis,

00:40:28.070 --> 00:40:30.080
if my goals change in a couple of days

00:40:30.080 --> 00:40:31.470
or a couple weeks, a couple months

00:40:31.470 --> 00:40:33.690
it doesn't completely rock my world

00:40:33.690 --> 00:40:36.330
because I know I'm
constantly moving forward.

00:40:36.330 --> 00:40:37.770
I think the problem becomes

00:40:37.770 --> 00:40:41.150
if you become so much of a goal
person or resolutions person

00:40:41.150 --> 00:40:43.662
but you're never actually
hitting any marks,

00:40:43.662 --> 00:40:46.350
that can be troubling,
that can be difficult.

00:40:46.350 --> 00:40:48.920
But I feel very much on
the same page as Gary

00:40:48.920 --> 00:40:52.000
that it's like, these are
helping me to make sure

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:54.080
I stay on point with my why,

00:40:54.080 --> 00:40:57.540
they're helping me to not get distracted

00:40:57.540 --> 00:41:00.040
by whatever I saw on
Instagram that looks cool.

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:01.610
Like, okay cool, that's my new goal.

00:41:01.610 --> 00:41:03.350
No, it's not, it's somebody else's

00:41:03.350 --> 00:41:05.130
and it's okay to be happy for them.

00:41:05.130 --> 00:41:09.090
But having something that I
know drives me to focus on

00:41:09.090 --> 00:41:10.300
keeps me focused every day

00:41:10.300 --> 00:41:12.030
so I'm not getting into the nitty-gritty

00:41:12.030 --> 00:41:13.750
and all the drama's of the day.

00:41:13.750 --> 00:41:17.180
But then really I'm happy
to take everything in stride

00:41:17.180 --> 00:41:18.260
the way that it goes.

00:41:18.260 --> 00:41:19.440
- [Gary] 'Cause that's fucking life.

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:21.360
- It is, like you can't change that.

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:23.760
- Crying about the fact that
you took on college debt

00:41:23.760 --> 00:41:26.150
because now you listen to me
you're like, fuck, he's right.

00:41:26.150 --> 00:41:27.630
Like--
- So now what?

00:41:27.630 --> 00:41:29.940
- There's no fucking time machine.

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:31.940
Like can we just fucking
go there once and for all,

00:41:31.940 --> 00:41:33.350
there is no time machine,

00:41:33.350 --> 00:41:34.980
you're not going into a time machine

00:41:34.980 --> 00:41:37.840
and every second you dwell about something

00:41:37.840 --> 00:41:41.020
that has already happened or
completely out of your control

00:41:41.020 --> 00:41:43.550
is a second you're not
using on the solution.

00:41:43.550 --> 00:41:45.910
- And also the news is just like loving

00:41:45.910 --> 00:41:47.210
that you're sitting on
this because they're like,

00:41:47.210 --> 00:41:50.700
hey, hey, we might
forgive all student debt,

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:52.710
we might and you could qualify.

00:41:52.710 --> 00:41:54.430
I'm not sitting here waiting

00:41:54.430 --> 00:41:56.680
to see how that works out for somebody.

00:41:56.680 --> 00:41:59.570
It's like, you've got to
make that change on your own

00:41:59.570 --> 00:42:01.440
and maybe if something
good happens along the way

00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:02.870
it's 'cause you were working so hard at it

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:03.790
on the day to day.

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:06.320
- Wiping out somebody's mistake

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:08.920
is the quickest way to make
sure they make it again.

00:42:11.170 --> 00:42:14.170
- [Calvin] Last question,
to Amy, if I can.

00:42:14.170 --> 00:42:15.830
- Please.
- Sure.

00:42:15.830 --> 00:42:17.680
